  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis strategy employs a genetic algorithm (GA) to explore the step function space for minimizing the overlap integral. By leveraging low-discrepancy Halton sequences for initialization and carefully designed genetic operators, we aim to escape local optima and find a superior solution. The approach balances exploration and exploitation, combining global search with local refinement.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points}, 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, \u2211h_i = n_points / 2\n- Objective: min_h max_k [\u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx] = min_h max(np.correlate(h, 1 - h, \"full\") * dx)\n- Genetic operators: Crossover (single-point), mutation (Gaussian perturbations), repair (constraint enforcement)\n- Fitness: Direct evaluation of overlap integral using np.correlate\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   a. If initial_h_values exist, interpolate to n_points and normalize.\n   b. Otherwise, generate Halton sequences for initial population.\n   c. Ensure each individual meets sum and bound constraints.\n\n2. Core iteration:\n   a. Compute fitness for all individuals.\n   b. Select parents using tournament selection.\n   c. Perform crossover and mutation to create offspring.\n   d. Repair offspring to enforce constraints.\n   e. Replace worst individuals with offspring.\n   f. Track best solution and adapt mutation rate.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Population size: 100 individuals\n- Initial mutation rate: 0.1\n- Crossover rate: 0.8\n- Generations: 600\n- Halton sequence initialization\n- Tournament selection size: 5\n- Mutation decay factor: 0.99 per 10 generations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Fitness computation dominates cost at O(n^2). Halton-based initialization ensures efficient exploration. Mutation decay balances exploration/exploitation.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final h validated for 0 \u2264 h_i \u22641 and sum(h) = n_points/2.\n- Fitness recomputed to confirm C5 value.\n- Robust handling of boundary conditions and numerical precision.",
